{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GKP7KzdycrXxr3hgyZ8XtV","type":"tool_result","content":"                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">On a summer Sunday afternoon, Nick Carraway is forcibly pulled off a train stopped at the desolate Valley of Ashes by Tom Buchanan, where they meet Myrtle Wilson at her husband George B. Wilson’s run-down garage beneath the billboard of Doctor T. J. Eckleburg; Tom and Myrtle then travel by train to New York, with Myrtle riding in a separate car to avoid detection, before taking a lavender taxicab to her apartment on the top floor of a building at 158th Street, where she buys a dog, changes into a cream chiffon dress, and hosts a drunken gathering with her sister Catherine and her photographer neighbor Mr. McKee and his wife; as alcohol flows freely and Myrtle transforms from a vital, earthy woman into a performative, haughty figure, she boasts of aspirations including a massage, a dog collar, and a wreath for her mother’s grave, while Catherine reveals Myrtle married George believing he was a gentleman—only to bitterly regret it—until Myrtle defiantly shouts Daisy’s name, provoking Tom to violently break her nose with a single blow, triggering screams, bloodied towels, and frantic scrambling among the women, after which Nick, overwhelmed and detached, leaves with Mr. McKee, who sleeps through the violence and later invites him to lunch, ending the night exhausted in the cold waiting room of Pennsylvania Station, staring at the morning Tribune.</div>\n                \n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Nick Carraway</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Tom Buchanan</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\