{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EUD2wkrUpWe49is4BWNF5P","type":"tool_result","content":"630:                <h2>📖 Plot Summary</h2>\n631:                <div style=\"margin-top: 1rem; line-height: 1.8; white-space: pre-wrap;\">Mrs. Sigourney Howard, recounting her experiences through a first-person retrospective lens, begins her story in West Egg, where she arrives as a newcomer seeking a fresh start. She quickly becomes entangled in the lives of her cousin Daisy Buchanan and her husband Tom, a wealthy but deeply flawed man whose racist rhetoric and violent tendencies are evident during a tense evening visit. The haunting image of Gatsby stretching toward a distant green light across the bay marks the beginning of her fascination with the enigmatic millionaire, whose lavish parties mask a profound longing for Daisy. As Mrs. Howard navigates the glittering yet hollow world of East Egg and West Egg, she forms a tentative connection with Jordan Baker, whose dishonesty and emotional detachment mirror the moral decay beneath the surface of high society. Her growing awareness of Gatsby’s obsession with Daisy—rooted in a past romance from 1917—reveals the tragic depth of his dream, built on reinvention and illusion.\n2717:                                <strong>Mrs. Sigourney Howard</strong>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8f5db840-93a5-4ef9-b9d1-4bb976edd216","uuid":"798edb1c-c925-4a68-ad6f-6296145aa59b","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"630:                <h2>📖 Plot Summary</h2>\n631:                <div style=\"margin-top: 1rem; line-height: 1.8; white-space: pre-wrap;\">Mrs. Sigourney Howard, recounting her experiences through a first-person retrospective lens, begins her story in West Egg, where she arrives as a newcomer seeking a fresh start. She quickly becomes entangled in the lives of her cousin Daisy Buchanan and her husband Tom, a wealthy but deeply flawed man whose racist rhetoric and violent tendencies are evident during a tense evening visit. The haunting image of Gatsby stretching toward a distant green light across the bay marks the beginning of her fascination with the enigmatic millionaire, whose lavish parties mask a profound longing for Daisy. As Mrs. Howard navigates the glittering yet hollow world of East Egg and West Egg, she forms a tentative connection with Jordan Baker, whose dishonesty and emotional detachment mirror the moral decay beneath the surface of high society. Her growing awareness of Gatsby’s obsession with Daisy—rooted in a past romance from 1917—reveals the tragic depth of his dream, built on reinvention and illusion.\n2717:                                <strong>Mrs. Sigourney Howard</strong>","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false}}
