{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016yJgBgLVcq6Q8egwNvBEMU","type":"tool_result","content":"633:The rising action unfolds through a series of escalating encounters that expose the fragility of Gatsby’s constructed identity. Mrs. Howard witnesses Gatsby’s elaborate reunion with Daisy, a moment charged with emotion and tension, where his idealized vision of her begins to fracture under the weight of reality. Her observations deepen as she learns of Gatsby’s origins as James Gatz, a poor farm boy transformed by Dan Cody’s influence, and his rise through dubious means, including his association with Meyer Wolfsheim, a criminal figure whose past is steeped in violence. The emotional climax arrives at the Plaza Hotel, where Tom Buchanan dismantles Gatsby’s carefully curated life, exposing his criminal ties and forcing Daisy to choose between her past and present. When Daisy, unable to fully renounce Tom, declares her love for Gatsby but refuses to say she never loved Tom, Gatsby’s dream collapses. The fatal accident that follows—Myrtle Wilson’s death by a yellow car—becomes the catalyst for tragedy, as Tom manipulates Wilson into believing Gatsby was the driver.\n965:                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter opens with a New York reporter arriving at Gatsby’s West Egg mansion one morning, seeking a statement after hearing rumors about him, though he does not fully understand Gatsby’s significance. The narrative then traces Gatsby’s origins as James Gatz, a poor farm boy from North Dakota, who, at seventeen, rowed out to Dan Cody’s yacht, the Tuolomee, anchored off Lake Superior, adopted the name Jay Gatsby, and spent five years aboard as steward, mate, skipper, secretary, and jailor during cross-continent travels. After Cody’s death in Boston, Ella Kaye legally deprived Gatsby of his $25,000 inheritance, yet he retained the refined habits and identity Cody instilled—including his aversion to alcohol despite being subjected to champagne being rubbed into his hair at parties. Weeks later, the narrator visits Gatsby’s mansion on a Sunday afternoon and is startled when Tom Buchanan, Mr. Sloane, and a woman in a brown riding-habit arrive on horseback; Gatsby nervously offers drinks and invites them to supper, but they subtly exclude him despite the woman’s initial invitation. Later, Tom and Daisy attend one of Gatsby’s parties, where Daisy is both fascinated and repelled by the vulgarity of the crowd; Gatsby dances with her, then confides in the narrator that she didn’t enjoy the evening and expresses his desperate wish for her to tell Tom she never loved him, so they can return to Louisville and restart their relationship as if five years had never passed, a dream rooted in the haunting memory of their first kiss under moonlight five years earlier, when Gatsby felt he had touched the divine.</div>\n973:                    <span class=\"tag\">James Gatz</span>\n2424:                                <div class=\"description\">Dan Cody is a complex figure who serves as a transformative mentor to young James Gatz, offering him a path to a new identity and refined lifestyle. Though he embodies the excesses of the frontier era...</div>\n2460:                                <strong>James Gatz</strong>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8f5db840-93a5-4ef9-b9d1-4bb976edd216","uuid":"ffe9c1f2-85b4-4597-ab06-96a8be25abd8","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"633:The rising action unfolds through a series of escalating encounters that expose the fragility of Gatsby’s constructed identity. Mrs. Howard witnesses Gatsby’s elaborate reunion with Daisy, a moment charged with emotion and tension, where his idealized vision of her begins to fracture under the weight of reality. Her observations deepen as she learns of Gatsby’s origins as James Gatz, a poor farm boy transformed by Dan Cody’s influence, and his rise through dubious means, including his association with Meyer Wolfsheim,