{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929","id":"msg_013GpqZiL6YvSbykdszKdYWh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"So \"James Gatz\" is listed as a separate supporting character! This is a critical false split. Gatsby is the main character, and James Gatz is his birth name - they should be aliases of the same person, not separate characters.\n\nLet me now check the pronunciation guide and narrator issue more carefully, and also check for Jordan Baker relationship error."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2773,"cache_read_input_tokens":46793,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2773,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8f5db840-93a5-4ef9-b9d1-4bb976edd216","uuid":"37afc57c-1a8d-4420-b0da-d05d2574867b"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929","id":"msg_013GpqZiL6YvSbykdszKdYWh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01ToYnvGeM9aaJf8CSjZ7Nhp","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"grep -A 10 \"Jordan\" /home/zacharymandrews/Tools/audiobook_agent/output/gatsby/report.html | grep -i \"relationship\\|tom\\|nick\" | head -10","description":"Check Jordan Baker's relationships"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2773,"cache_read_input_tokens":46793,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2773,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8f5db840-93a5-4ef9-b9d1-4bb976edd216","uuid":"b7ef1646-0319-4798-bcb7-027f348d2e38"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ToYnvGeM9aaJf8CSjZ7Nhp","type":"tool_result","content":"                <div style=\"margin-top: 1rem; line-height: 1.8; white-space: pre-wrap;\">Mrs. Sigourney Howard, recounting her experiences through a first-person retrospective lens, begins her story in West Egg, where she arrives as a newcomer seeking a fresh start. She quickly becomes entangled in the lives of her cousin Daisy Buchanan and her husband Tom, a wealthy but deeply flawed man whose racist rhetoric and violent tendencies are evident during a tense evening visit. The haunting image of Gatsby stretching toward a distant green light across the bay marks the beginning of her fascination with the enigmatic millionaire, whose lavish parties mask a profound longing for Daisy. As Mrs. Howard navigates the glittering yet hollow world of East Egg and West Egg, she forms a tentative connection with Jordan Baker, whose dishonesty and emotional detachment mirror the moral decay beneath the surface of high society. Her growing awareness of Gatsby’s obsession with Daisy—rooted in a past romance from 1917—reveals the tragic depth of his dream, built on reinvention and illusion.\nThe rising action unfolds through a series of escalating encounters that expose the fragility of Gatsby’s constructed identity. Mrs. Howard witnesses Gatsby’s elaborate reunion with Daisy, a moment charged with emotion and tension, where his idealized vision of her begins to fracture under the weight of reality. Her observations deepen as she learns of Gatsby’s origins as James Gatz, a poor farm boy transformed by Dan Cody’s influence, and his rise through dubious means, including his association with Meyer Wolfsheim, a criminal figure whose past is steeped in violence. The emotional climax arrives at the Plaza Hotel, where Tom Buchanan dismantles Gatsby’s carefully curated life, exposing his criminal ties and forcing Daisy to choose between her past and present. When Daisy, unable to fully renounce Tom, declares her love for Gatsby but refuses to say she never loved Tom, Gatsby’s dream collapses. The fatal accident that follows—Myrtle Wilson’s death by a yellow car—becomes the catalyst for tragedy, as Tom manipulates Wilson into believing Gatsby was the driver.\n                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter opens with the narrator, Nick Carraway, arriving by car at a modest rental home in West Egg, where he feels a sense of belonging after helping a stranger find his way; he purchases twelve volumes on banking and investment as symbols of new wealth and ambition. He observes the stark contrast between West Egg and the opulent East Egg, where his cousin Daisy Buchanan and her husband Tom live in a Georgian Colonial mansion. During an evening visit, Nick is introduced to Daisy, whose voice captivates him, and Jordan Baker, a detached young woman; Tom’s domineering presence is underscored by his racist rant about 'The Rise of the Coloured Empires' and Daisy’s bruised knuckle, hinting at marital tension. Later, on a rosy-coloured porch at dusk with four candles flickering, Daisy snaps out the candles, mocks Tom’s views, and whispers a bizarre anecdote about the butler’s nose after a phone call interrupts them; Tom exits abruptly, returns with forced cheerfulness, and Daisy confides in Nick her cynical wish that her daughter be a 'beautiful little fool,' a statement Nick perceives as hollow. The evening ends with Jordan reading from the Saturday Evening Post in a crimson-lit room before departing, while Daisy and Tom press Nick about rumors of his engagement to a woman out West and warn him not to believe everything he hears. As Nick drives back to West Egg, he pauses in his yard to see Gatsby standing alone on the lawn, stretching his arms toward a distant green light across the bay before vanishing into the night.</div>\n                    <span class=\"tag\">Nick Carraway</span>\n                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">On a July morning, Gatsby arrives at the narrator’s door in his luxurious cream-colored car with a three-note horn, inviting him to lunch and revealing an elaborate, suspiciously theatrical biography—including Midwestern wealth, Oxford education, European aristocratic pursuits, and wartime heroism, backed by a Montenegrin medal and a photograph from Trinity Quad. During the drive past Port Roosevelt, the valley of ashes, a funeral procession, and a limousine with African American passengers, Gatsby stops a policeman with a card to demonstrate his influence, then hints at a secret request involving Jordan Baker. At noon in a cellar restaurant on Forty-second Street, Gatsby introduces the narrator to Meyer Wolfshiem, a grotesque figure with hair in his nostrils and cuff buttons made of human molars, who recounts the 1919 murder of Rosy Rosenthal with sentimental detail; Gatsby abruptly leaves to make a phone call and returns to share a strained handshake with Tom Buchanan before vanishing. Later, Jordan reveals to the narrator that Gatsby bought his mansion solely to be near Daisy, whom he had loved in 1917 Louisville, where she was seen in a white roadster with him as a lieutenant; she later married Tom Buchanan after a drunken, chaotic wedding day in 1919, during which she clutched a letter and the three-hundred-fifty-thousand-dollar pearl necklace Tom gave her. On a rainy afternoon, Gatsby nervously prepares for Daisy’s tea by having the narrator’s lawn mowed and sending an entire greenhouse of flowers, arriving in a white flannel suit and silver shirt, trembling as Daisy arrives in a large open car during the storm; he freezes upon seeing her, nearly knocking over a mantel clock, and their reunion is stiff and awkward until the narrator leaves them alone. Upon returning, he finds Gatsby transformed—radiant and awestruck—as Daisy admires his mansion, which he reveals he built in three years through unspecified business ventures, and bursts into tears over his collection of luxurious shirts. As rain clears, Gatsby points out the green light at Daisy’s dock, now diminished in symbolic power, shows her photographs of Mr. Dan Cody and his youthful self in yachting gear, and displays clippings about her; after a frustrated phone call about a 'small town,' he summons Klipspringer to play piano, and Gatsby and Daisy sit in shadowed intimacy, emotionally reconnected, while the narrator departs, noticing Gatsby’s dazed bewilderment as his idealized vision of Daisy begins to crack.</div>\n                    <span class=\"tag\">Tom Buchanan</span>\n                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">On the hottest day of summer, the narrator arrives by train at the Buchanans’ house to find Gatsby waiting, having dismissed his staff and replaced them with Wolfsheim’s silent relatives; Daisy openly kisses Gatsby and declares her love, shocking Tom, who immediately begins questioning Gatsby’s background. The group departs for New York in two cars—Gatsby driving his own vehicle, Tom taking Daisy in the coupé—stopping at Wilson’s garage in the valley of ashes, where Wilson, visibly broken, reveals his plan to flee with Myrtle and offers to buy Tom’s old car, while Myrtle watches from the window, mistaking Jordan for Tom’s wife in a moment of jealous terror beneath the faded eyes of Doctor T. J. Eckleburg. At the Plaza Hotel, a volatile argument erupts in a stifling suite as Tom accuses Gatsby of bootlegging and lying about Oxford; Daisy reluctantly admits she once loved Tom but now loves Gatsby, yet refuses to say she never loved Tom, shattering Gatsby’s idealized vision. Tom orders Daisy and Gatsby to leave together in Gatsby’s car, ending the confrontation. Later, Myrtle Wilson rushes out into the dusk and is struck and killed by a speeding yellow car that flees the scene; Michaelis and others find her body mangled, with a torn left breast and split mouth. Tom and the narrator arrive at the garage to find Wilson hysterical, crying 'Oh, my God!' as Tom falsely reassures him the yellow car was not his. That night, Gatsby emerges from the shadows outside the Buchanan house in his pink suit, confessing to Nick that Daisy was driving the car that killed Myrtle but insisting he will take the blame; Nick finds Tom and Daisy calmly eating cold chicken together in the kitchen, their posture intimate and conspiratorial, while Gatsby stands vigil in the moonlight, refusing to leave, watching over nothing.</div>\n                    <span class=\"tag\">Nick Carraway</span>\n                    <span class=\"tag\">Tom Buchanan</span>\n                    <div class=\"profile-body\">Daisy Buchanan is a woman of apparent privilege and social prominence, residing in East Egg with her husband Tom. She displays a mix of emotional detachment and moments of vulnerability, particularly in private moments with Nick and Jordan. Her behavior shifts from playful and flirtatious to emotionally strained, especially during confrontations involving her husband and others, suggesting inner conflict and emotional complexity.</div>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8f5db840-93a5-4ef9-b9d1-4bb976edd216","uuid":"830f1b2a-ca75-47db-9a29-ba5a6b65dfe0","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"                <div style=\"margin-top: 1rem; line-height: 1.8; white-space: pre-wrap;\">Mrs. Sigourney Howard, recounting her experiences through a first-person retrospective lens, begins her story in West Egg, where she arrives as a newcomer seeking a fresh start. She quickly becomes entangled in the lives of her cousin Daisy Buchanan and her husband Tom, a wealthy but deeply flawed man whose racist rhetoric and violent tendencies are evident during a tense evening visit. The haunting image of Gatsby stretching toward a distant green light across the bay marks the beginning of her fascination with the enigmatic millionaire, whose lavish parties mask a profound longing for Daisy. As Mrs. Howard navigates the glittering yet hollow world of East Egg and West Egg, she forms a tentative connection with Jordan Baker, whose dishonesty and emotional detachment mirror the moral decay beneath the surface of high society. Her growing awareness of Gatsby’s obsession with Daisy—rooted in a past romance from 1917—reveals the tragic depth of his dream, built on reinvention and illusion.\nThe rising action unfolds through a series of escalating encounters that expose the fragility of Gatsby’s constructed identity. Mrs. Howard witnesses Gatsby’s elaborate reunion with Daisy, a moment charged with emotion and tension, where his idealized vision of her begins to fracture under the weight of reality. Her observations deepen as she learns of Gatsby’s origins as James Gatz, a poor farm boy transformed by Dan Cody’s influence, and his rise through dubious means, including his association with Meyer Wolfsheim, a criminal figure whose past is steeped in violence. The emotional climax arrives at the Plaza Hotel, where Tom Buchanan dismantles Gatsby’s carefully curated life, exposing his criminal ties and forcing Daisy to choose between her past and present. When Daisy, unable to fully renounce Tom, declares her love for Gatsby but refuses to say she never loved Tom, Gatsby’s dream collapses. The fatal accident that follows—Myrtle Wilson’s death by a yellow car—becomes the catalyst for tragedy, as Tom manipulates Wilson into believing Gatsby was the driver.\n                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter opens with the narrator, Nick Carraway, arriving by car at a modest rental home in West Egg, where he feels a sense of belonging after helping a stranger find his way; he purchases twelve volumes on banking and investment as symbols of new wealth and ambition. He observes the stark contrast between West Egg and the opulent East Egg, where his cousin Daisy Buchanan and her husband Tom live in a Georgian Colonial mansion. During an evening visit, Nick is introduced to Daisy, whose voice captivates him, and Jordan Baker, a detached young woman; Tom’s domineering presence is underscored by his racist rant about 'The Rise of the Coloured Empires' and Daisy’s bruised knuckle, hinting at marital tension. Later, on a rosy-coloured porch at dusk with four candles flickering, Daisy snaps out the candles, mocks Tom’s views, and whispers a bizarre anecdote about the butler’s nose after a phone call interrupts them; Tom exits abruptly, returns with forced cheerfulness, and Daisy confides in Nick her cynical wish that her daughter be a 'beautiful little fool,' a statement Nick perceives as hollow. The evening ends with Jordan reading from the Saturday Evening Post in a crimson-lit room before departing, while Daisy and Tom press Nick about rumors of his engagement to a woman out West and warn him not to believe everything he hears. As Nick drives back to West Egg, he pauses in his yard to see Gatsby standing alone on the lawn, stretching his arms toward a distant green light across the bay before vanishing into the night.</div>\n                    <span class=\"tag\">Nick Carraway</span>\n                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">On a July morning, Gatsby arrives at the narrator’s door in his luxurious cream-colored car with a three-note horn, inviting him to lunch and revealing an elaborate, suspiciously theatrical biography—including Midwestern wealth, Oxford education, European aristocratic pursuits, and wartime heroism, backed by a Montenegrin medal and a photograph from Trinity Quad. During the drive past Port Roosevelt, the valley of ashes, a funeral procession, and a limousine with African American passengers, Gatsby stops a policeman with a card to demonstrate his influence, then hints at a secret request involving Jordan Baker. At noon in a cellar restaurant on Forty-second Street, Gatsby introduces the narrator to Meyer Wolfshiem, a grotesque figure with hair in his nostrils and cuff buttons made of human molars, who recounts the 1919 murder of Rosy Rosenthal with sentimental detail; Gatsby abruptly leaves to make a phone call and returns to share a strained handshake with Tom Buchanan before vanishing. Later, Jordan reveals to the narrator that Gatsby bought his mansion solely to be near Daisy, whom he had loved in 1917 Louisville, where she was seen in a white roadster with him as a lieutenant; she later married Tom Buchanan after a drunken, chaotic wedding day in 1919, during which she clutched a letter and the three-hundred-fifty-thousand-dollar pearl necklace Tom gave her. On a rainy afternoon, Gatsby nervously prepares for Daisy’s tea by having the narrator’s lawn mowed and sending an entire greenhouse of flowers, arriving in a white flannel suit and silver shirt, trembling as Daisy arrives in a large open car during the storm; he freezes upon seeing her, nearly knocking over a mantel clock, and their reunion is stiff and awkward until the narrator leaves them alone. Upon returning, he finds Gatsby transformed—radiant and awestruck—as Daisy admires his mansion, which he reveals he built in three years through unspecified business ventures, and bursts into tears over his collection of luxurious shirts. As rain clears, Gatsby points out the green light at Daisy’s dock, now diminished in symbolic power, shows her photographs of Mr. Dan Cody and his youthful self in yachting gear, and displays clippings about her; after a frustrated phone call about a 'small town,' he summons Klipspringer to play piano, and Gatsby and Daisy sit in shadowed intimacy, emotionally reconnected, while the narrator departs, noticing Gatsby’s dazed bewilderment as his idealized vision of Daisy begins to crack.</div>\n                    <span class=\"tag\">Tom Buchanan</span>\n                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">On the hottest day of summer, the narrator arrives by train at the Buchanans’ house to find Gatsby waiting, having dismissed his staff and replaced them with Wolfsheim’s silent relatives; Daisy openly kisses Gatsby and declares her love, shocking Tom, who immediately begins questioning Gatsby’s background. The group departs for New York in two cars—Gatsby driving his own vehicle, Tom taking Daisy in the coupé—stopping at Wilson’s garage in the valley of ashes, where Wilson, visibly broken, reveals his plan to flee with Myrtle and offers to buy Tom’s old car, while Myrtle watches from the window, mistaking Jordan for Tom’s wife in a moment of jealous terror beneath the faded eyes of Doctor T. J. Eckleburg. At the Plaza Hotel, a volatile argument erupts in a stifling suite as Tom accuses Gatsby of bootlegging and lying about Oxford; Daisy reluctantly admits she once loved Tom but now loves Gatsby, yet refuses to say she never loved Tom, shattering Gatsby’s idealized vision. Tom orders Daisy and Gatsby to leave together in Gatsby’s car, ending the confrontation. Later, Myrtle Wilson rushes out into the dusk and is struck and killed by a speeding yellow car that flees the scene; Michaelis and others find her body mangled, with a torn left breast and split mouth. Tom and the narrator arrive at the garage to find Wilson hysterical, crying 'Oh, my God!' as Tom falsely reassures him the yellow car was not his. That night, Gatsby emerges from the shadows outside the Buchanan house in his pink suit, confessing to Nick that Daisy was driving the car that killed Myrtle but insisting he will take the blame; Nick finds Tom and Daisy calmly eating cold chicken together in the kitchen, their posture intimate and conspiratorial, while Gatsby stands vigil in the moonlight, refusing to leave, watching over nothing.</div>\n                    <span class=\"tag\">Nick Carraway</span>\n                    <span class=\"tag\">Tom Buchanan</span>\n                    <div class=\"profile-body\">Daisy Buchanan is a woman of apparent privilege and social prominence, residing in East Egg with her husband Tom. She displays a mix of emotional detachment and moments of vulnerability, particularly in private moments with Nick and Jordan. Her behavior shifts from playful and flirtatious to emotionally strained, especially during confrontations involving her husband and others, suggesting inner conflict and emotional complexity.</div>","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false}}
